Files in this folder are based on libxml2 2.15.4.

Original source: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/libxml2, tag v2.15.4.

Only the library is kept: its sources (and libxml.h and timsort.h) in src/,
the generated tables they include in src/codegen/, the private headers in
src/include/private/ (where they find src/libxml.h), the public headers in
include/libxml/, and Copyright and README.md in docs/, renamed COPYING and
README. xmllint, xmlcatalog, the tests, the Python bindings and the build
files are left out, and so is xmlmodule.c (module loading is off).

HTTP and FTP support, which 2.15 and 2.14 removed, come from the last
releases that had them (tags v2.14.6 and v2.13.9 of the same repository):
src/nanohttp.c and src/wsockcompat.h are 2.14.6's, src/nanoftp.c and
include/libxml/nanoftp.h are 2.13.9's. They are restored so that revXML
loads http:// and ftp:// URLs (revXMLCreateTreeFromFile, the XSLT *File
functions, document() and xsl:include) as it did with 2.9.4: plain HTTP
and FTP only, as before, HTTP redirects followed, and through the proxy
that http_proxy or ftp_proxy names unless no_proxy says otherwise. (For
HTTP, no_proxy may list hosts since 2.11; 2.9 knew only "*", and FTP
still does.)

include/libxml/xmlversion.h is made from xmlversion.h.in with the features
LiveCode's 2.9.4 build had, less modules (off); src/config.h is written
for OXT-Beyond's build (gyp runs no configure step).

Local changes, marked "OXT-Beyond" in the sources:

- From LiveCode (bug 7586): xmlSAX2StartElementNoNS in src/SAX2.c
  (declared in include/libxml/SAX2.h), used by revXML when namespace
  processing is off. It is xmlSAX2StartElement without splitting element
  and attribute names at their prefix.
- In src/parser.c, a parser with a SAX1 handler (revXML's) passes the
  start tag's line to xmlParseEndTag1, so "Opening and ending tag
  mismatch: b line 2 and c" names the line, as 2.9 did, not line 0.
- The HTTP and FTP clients above, built with 2.15's public headers: the
  xmlIOHTTP* and xmlIOFTP* input callbacks, which 2.14.6 and 2.13.9 kept
  in xmlIO.c, are at the end of each file; their errors are not reported
  (2.14.6's nanohttp.c already did not; 2.9 printed them to stderr);
  nanohttp.c waits with poll() on POSIX systems, as 2.9's build did; in
  nanoftp.c, each platform's headers and getaddrinfo, have_ipv6 closes
  its socket with closesocket(), no select() on a socket numbered
  FD_SETSIZE or above, and EPRT sends the port in decimal.
- In include/libxml/xmlversion.h, LIBXML_HTTP_ENABLED and
  LIBXML_FTP_ENABLED; include/libxml/nanohttp.h and xmlIO.h declare the
  HTTP functions for them (2.15 for its stubs only), and xmlIO.h the FTP
  input callbacks.
- In src/xmlIO.c, xmlParserInputBufferCreateUrl opens http:// and ftp://
  URLs with the clients, where 2.14.6's xmlInputDefaultOpen opened HTTP:
  after any callbacks the application registered, and only for a parser
  without XML_PARSE_NONET. A URL that fails to load is XML_IO_ENOENT, a
  warning as with 2.9, and is not then tried as a file name.
- In src/parserInternals.c, 2.14.6's HTTP checks of a new input
  (xmlCheckHTTPInputInternal, and the deprecated xmlCheckHTTPInput, which
  2.15 made return NULL): an error status fails the load (XML_IO_ENOENT,
  where 2.14.6 had a fatal XML_IO_LOAD_ERROR), the Content-Type charset of
  an XML media type is the input's encoding unless it is UTF-8 (setting
  one makes 2.15 ignore the XML declaration's, which 2.9 still honoured
  when it was not UTF-8) or unknown, and a redirect's target is the
  input's file name, the base URI. On Windows, xmlNewInputFromUrl turns
  "http:\\host\doc.xml", the path form of a URL that revXML passes there,
  back into "http://host/doc.xml", as 2.9's xmlCanonicPath did.

LiveCode's other changes to 2.9.4 are not needed: revXML now parses
through a SAX parser context with xmlCtxtReadMemory and xmlCtxtReadFile
instead of the two parse functions LiveCode added, and the rest were
fixes for old compilers. The parse is not quite the same as 2.9's: with
XML_PARSE_HUGE, 2.15 still refuses elements nested more than 2048 deep and
text nodes, attribute values or names over 1,000,000,000 bytes, where 2.9
had no limit, and it reads a gzip-compressed file only with
XML_PARSE_UNZIP, which revXML passes for files.
